window.PH_BOOK_LOAD({"id":"17-11","translated":true,"translator":"AI (Claude)","sections":[{"slug":"17-11-01","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/17-11-01/","title_he":"א – טעם מצוות חלה מהלחם","title_en":"The Reason for the Mitzva of Ḥalla from Bread","body_en":"<p>It is a mitzva to set aside from dough intended for making bread a gift of ḥalla for the kohen, and the kohen and his family members make breads and cakes from it and eat them in purity, so that they can fulfill their spiritual mission of teaching Torah to Israel. As it is stated (Bamidbar 15:17-21): \"And the Lord spoke to Moshe, saying: Speak to the Children of Israel and say to them: When you come into the land to which I am bringing you, then it shall be that when you eat of the bread of the land, you shall set apart a gift to the Lord. Of the first of your dough you shall set apart a cake (ḥalla) as a gift; as the gift of the threshing floor, so shall you set it apart. Of the first of your dough you shall give to the Lord a gift throughout your generations.\"</p><p>Bread, which is man's principal food, expresses to a great extent the special character of man, who has the ability to choose, to create and to perfect. For all animals eat natural foods, such as grasses, leaves, grains, vegetables, fruits and even meat, whereas man eats bread, whose process of preparation is long and complex and requires the performance of many labors: plowing the soil, sowing the grain, reaping the ears, separating the grain kernels from the ears and the chaff through threshing, winnowing and sorting, grinding the kernels into flour, kneading it with water to make it into dough, and baking it. Of all the foods that man eats, the production process of bread is the most complex and sophisticated. As with bread, so with man: all the animals in the world learn, in a short time and naturally, how to survive and to be fruitful and multiply, whereas man needs to learn over many years how to obtain food, clothing and a dwelling for himself and to establish a family. And in the course of this he learns to harness the immense forces of nature to his service. The ability to choose, learn, create and perfect is the expression of the Divine image in man.</p><p>However, man may turn his powers to evil – to be haughty toward his fellows, to lie and steal, to destroy and betray – in order to increase his desires. Through the prohibition of eating bread from which ḥalla has not been separated, a person remembers God, who created all his creative powers, and takes care not to use them for evil. Through the mitzva of giving the ḥalla to the kohen, the bread and the process of its preparation become connected to holiness, and those who eat from it can elevate and direct their creative power toward goodness and blessing. This is what the Sages said (Vayikra Rabba 15:6): \"Whoever fulfills the mitzva of ḥalla, it is as if he has abolished idolatry, and whoever neglects the mitzva of ḥalla, it is as if he has upheld idolatry.\" The broad meaning of the concept \"idolatry\" is all the evil deeds that a person does for the idols of his money, his lust and his pride.</p><p>The Sages further said that man himself is \"the ḥalla of the world\": the Holy One, blessed be He, created the earth and all the materials within it, kneaded it with the rainwater, and from the \"dough\" that was formed He created all the plants and animals, and from within the \"dough\" He separated \"ḥalla,\" and from it He created man in His image, so that he would lead the world for the good. And when Adam sinned, he rendered himself and the whole world impure. In order to rectify his sin, Israel was commanded to separate ḥalla for the kohen and to keep it in purity, in order to remember their destiny. And this mitzva belongs more to the woman, for she has more power to direct the forces within man and thereby rectify the sin (see Bereshit Rabba 14:1; 17:7-8).</p><p>The Sages said (there 1:4): \"In the merit of three things the world was created: in the merit of ḥalla, in the merit of ma'asrot, and in the merit of bikkurim.\" For these three mitzvot are called \"reshit\" (first), and they remind us that the first aim of creation is to reveal holiness within the world. Bikkurim and terumot and ma'asrot are given from the produce that grows naturally. In the merit of the bikkurim, one connects to the overall vision of the people of Israel (above 7:15), and in the merit of terumot and ma'asrot, the blessing spreads to the fields and the crops (above 7:13). The ḥalla is given from the work of a person's hands, and in its merit he merits that blessing rest upon the work of his hands and upon his home, as it is stated (Yeḥezkel 44:30): \"And the first of your dough you shall give to the kohen – to cause a blessing to rest upon your house.\"</p>","notes_en":[]},{"slug":"17-11-02","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/17-11-02/","title_he":"ב – ייחודה של החלה","title_en":"The Uniqueness of Ḥalla","body_en":"<p>Ḥalla is one of the twenty-four gifts that Israel was commanded to give to the kohanim, so that they would be free of the burden of earning a livelihood and could fulfill their sacred mission of educating Israel in Torah, mitzvot and good character traits. Among all the gifts, the gift of \"ḥalla\" had special importance, for from it the kohanim prepared bread, which is man's principal food. In addition, through the mitzva of ḥalla a continuous connection was created between the Yisraelim and the kohanim. Unlike terumot and ma'asrot from fruit and grain, which the owners of the fields would distribute in the fields in large quantities several times a year, ḥalla would be given every single day, from each Israelite woman to her neighbor, the wife of a kohen, and thus all of Israel would connect to sacred values while preparing their bread.</p><p>When ḥalla is separated, the ḥalla becomes sanctified like terumah: it is forbidden for Yisraelim and Levites to eat it, and the kohanim must be careful to eat it in purity, as it is stated (Bamidbar 15:20): \"as the gift of the threshing floor, so shall you set it apart.\" Therefore, the Yisraelim must be very careful not to render it impure, for if it becomes impure, it becomes forbidden to eat. And there was a difficulty in this, because the rule is that produce can become impure only after it has been made wet with water, in order to wash it or to prepare it as food or for another purpose; but produce that has not yet been made wet cannot become impure. Therefore, there is no problem for the Yisraelim to separate terumot from produce, since they separate them from produce that has not been made wet. But in the mitzva of ḥalla, one becomes obligated only after mixing the flour with water and making dough from it. And if the one preparing the dough is impure, he renders the dough impure with his hands and causes the ḥalla to be impure, and thereby he disgraces the mitzva and causes the loss of the ḥalla. Therefore, when the woman who kneaded the dough was impure because of her nidda or other impurities, she would call her neighbor, the wife of a kohen, to knead the dough for her, and the Israelite woman would recite the blessing over separating the ḥalla, and the kohen's wife would take her ḥalla (Yerushalmi Ḥalla 3:1). In this way the connection between the families of the Yisraelim and the families of the kohanim grew very strong.<sup>1</sup></p><p>By Torah law ḥalla has no fixed measure, but the Sages instituted that every person should separate at least one twenty-fourth of the dough (about 4%), so that his giving would have significance. And a baker who earns his living from baking bread, since he is stingy with the gift he must give the kohen, should give at least one forty-eighth (about 2%). And since his dough is usually large, even one forty-eighth amounts to a fine gift. When the dough became impure unintentionally, even a person at home should separate the lower measure, one forty-eighth, since in any case the kohen cannot eat it (Mishna Ḥalla 1:9; 2:7; Yerushalmi 2:3; SA YD 328:1).</p>","notes_en":[{"n":1,"en":"Since ḥalla and terumah must be eaten in purity, it is forbidden to render terumah and ḥalla impure or to cause them a loss (Rambam, Hilkhot Terumot 12:1). In order to help the kohanim sustain themselves during their days of impurity, the Torah established additional gifts that may be eaten in impurity, such as the foreleg, the cheeks and the maw (above 7:4; below 19:1). When the Yisrael is impure, and within a distance of less than four mil there is a pure kohen who can knead the dough, according to the Rambam (Hilkhot Bikkurim 8:11) he must take the trouble to bring him the dough so that he will knead it in purity. Some say that this is the law for one traveling on his way (Tosafot, Pesaḥim 46a, s.v. \"legabei\"). And according to most poskim this is the law also for one who is staying in his place – that up to four mil he must take the trouble to have the dough kneaded in purity (Maharik Korkos, Kesef Mishneh and others). When the pure kohen's location is farther away, or he cannot go to the kohen, some say that he should knead less than the measure that obligates ḥalla each time (Ramban and Rashba), and some say that he should knead in impurity and the ḥalla will be impure (Rambam).<br>A Yisrael or an impure kohen who ate terumah or ḥalla is liable to death at the hands of Heaven (Mishna Ḥalla 1:9; Sanhedrin 83a), as it is stated (Vayikra 22:9): \"They shall keep My charge, lest they bear sin for it and die thereby, if they profane it; I am the Lord who sanctifies them.\" The same is the law of a kohen or a Yisrael who intentionally ate a baked item that is obligated in ḥalla by Torah law and from which ḥalla was not separated (Rambam, Hilkhot Bikkurim 8:5). If the terumah or ḥalla was impure, a kohen who ate it is not liable to death at the hands of Heaven (Rambam, Hilkhot Terumot 7:1). And some hold that he is liable to death at the hands of Heaven (Rashi, Yevamot 91a, s.v. \"teruma\"; and the Rash based on Mishna Para 11:3)."}]},{"slug":"17-11-03","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/17-11-03/","title_he":"ג – המצווה בארץ ובחוץ לארץ","title_en":"The Mitzva in the Land and Outside the Land","body_en":"<p>The mitzva of ḥalla, like the mitzva of terumot and ma'asrot, is dependent on the Land, as it is stated (Bamidbar 15:18-19): \"When you come into the land to which I am bringing you … when you eat of the bread of the land, you shall set apart a gift to the Lord.\" An additional condition in these mitzvot is that the majority of Israel reside in the Land, as it is stated (there 18): \"When you come (be-vo'akhem) into the land\" – \"be-vo'akhem\" means \"when all of you come, and not when some of you come\" (Ketubot 25a). We learn a great principle from this: the ability to reveal holiness in nature depends on the people of Israel, who were created in order to reveal the word of God, living in the Land of Israel, which was created so that holiness would be revealed through it.</p><p>However, there is a difference between the mitzvot: the mitzva of terumot and ma'asrot applies to produce that grew in the Land and whose processing was completed in the Land, even if it is taken out of the Land (above 8:9), whereas the mitzva of ḥalla depends on the kneading of the dough: if the dough was kneaded in the Land, even if the flour is from grain grown outside the Land, one must separate ḥalla from it; and if the dough was kneaded outside the Land, even if the flour is from grain of the Land of Israel, it is exempt from the mitzva of ḥalla (Yerushalmi Ḥalla 2:1). In the course of this we learn that there is special value to the creations that Israel creates in the Land of Israel, even when the materials were brought from outside the Land, and therefore they must be sanctified by setting aside ḥalla.</p><p>Following the exile of Israel in the days of the destruction of the First Temple, the majority of Israel was no longer in the Land, and the mitzva lapsed. Even in the days of the building of the Second Temple, the mitzva did not return to its place, since the majority of Israel remained in exile. However, the Sages of the Great Beit Din established at the beginning of the Second Temple period, headed by Ezra the Scribe, instituted that even when the majority of Israel is not in its Land, people would be obligated by rabbinic law to separate terumot, ma'asrot and ḥalla. They further instituted that even Jews living outside the Land would be obligated to separate ḥalla from their dough, so that the mitzva of ḥalla would not be forgotten by them. And why did they institute this for ḥalla and not for terumot and ma'asrot? Because the mitzva of ḥalla resembles to a certain extent the mitzvot that depend on the person and not on the land, since one becomes obligated in it at the time one kneads the dough (Tosafot, Kiddushin 36b; below 12:10). One can learn a profound idea from this enactment: in the merit of Israel's having fulfilled the mitzva of ḥalla in the Land of Israel throughout the days of the First Temple, the Sages were able to extend the obligation of the mitzva and the revelation of holiness to the dough that Israel makes outside the Land, and thereby connect all the creative activities that Jews perform outside the Land to the vision of the revelation of holiness that spreads from the Land to the entire world.</p>","notes_en":[]},{"slug":"17-11-04","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/17-11-04/","title_he":"ד – מצוות חלה בימינו","title_en":"The Mitzva of Ḥalla in Our Day","body_en":"<p>Following the destruction of the Second Temple and the dwindling of the Jewish community in the Land, the ability to become purified by means of the water of the ashes of the red heifer ceased, and consequently all kohanim are considered impure from contact with the dead, and it is forbidden for them to eat terumah and ḥalla (above 7:1). Nevertheless, the obligation of separating ḥalla remains in force, and the ḥalla is burned or buried in a place where people will not stumble by eating it. And as long as ḥalla has not been separated, the bread or cakes are forbidden to be eaten (the law outside the Land will be explained below, halakha 13). However, since in any case the ḥalla goes to waste, there is no need to separate one twenty-fourth of the dough as ḥalla; rather, it suffices to separate any amount whatsoever.</p><p>And although the mitzva today is rabbinic and the kohanim may not eat the ḥalla, through the mitzva of separating ḥalla we remember the vision, and our bread becomes connected to sacred values, and blessing is drawn into our homes. Therefore, some women have the custom to beautify the mitzva and bake an amount that obligates ḥalla on Erev Shabbat (Rema OḤ 242:1).</p><p>Regarding outside the Land: at the beginning of the Second Temple period, in accordance with the Sages' enactment, they would separate ḥalla outside the Land and eat it in purity. After several generations, the Sages decreed impurity upon the lands of the nations (below 12:9), and consequently the ḥalla separated outside the Land becomes impure and it is forbidden for kohanim to eat it. And so that people would not forget that ḥalla is intended to be eaten by the kohanim, the Sages instituted separating two ḥallot outside the Land: the first is burned, according to the law of impure ḥalla, which is forbidden to be eaten, and the second is eaten by the kohanim even when they are impure. Over time, in most communities of the Diaspora, the custom became accepted to separate only one ḥalla, and it is burned or buried. For the basis of the enactment of a second ḥalla outside the Land is to remember that ḥalla is intended to be eaten, as is practiced in the Land of Israel. And when in the Land of Israel the kohanim may not eat the ḥalla because of impurity, it is not fitting that outside the Land the kohanim should eat the ḥalla, and consequently outside the Land too one separates one ḥalla for burning (Rema OḤ 322:5 [?]; below 12:10).<sup>2</sup></p><p>In summary: even today, when the kohanim may not eat the ḥalla, there is a rabbinic obligation upon every Jew, whether in the Land or outside the Land, to separate any amount whatsoever from the dough as ḥalla. When the majority of Israel lives in the Land, the obligation of the mitzva in the Land will once again be of Torah origin and blessing will increase, but it will still be forbidden for kohanim to eat the ḥalla because of impurity. When we merit the return of the order of purity to Israel, the kohanim will be able to reveal holiness in their lives and eat the ḥalla, and the blessing will grow stronger in Israel until it spreads openly to the entire world, and all the families of the earth will be blessed through us. And the allusion to the spreading of blessing to the entire world is in the enactment that the Sages instituted to separate ḥalla outside the Land as well.</p>","notes_en":[{"n":2,"en":"From the words of the Rambam (Hilkhot Bikkurim 5:10-11) and SA 322:5 it is implied that the law outside the Land has not changed, and even today it is a mitzva to separate two ḥallot outside the Land, and the second is eaten by impure kohanim, even with an impurity that emanates from their bodies, such as a seminal emission or nidda. And when there is a kohen there who is pure of impurity emanating from his body, one may separate one ḥalla and he eats it. However, the Rash, Rashba and Semag wrote that outside the Land the custom is to separate one ḥalla for burning, since in the Land of Israel too the ḥalla is not eaten. This custom is cited in Tosafot (Ḥullin 104a, s.v. \"ḥallat\") and in Sefer Ha-teruma. An additional reason for the abolition of the custom of eating ḥalla outside the Land is lest people not treat it properly and eat it in a forbidden manner (Ha-teruma, Radbaz). So too wrote the Rema 322:5 and MB 457:22, that the custom in all Ashkenazic lands is to separate one ḥalla for burning. The prevalent Sephardic custom as well is not to eat ḥalla outside the Land, as the Radbaz wrote (3:864). So too wrote Tevu'at Ha-sadeh 2:8 and Kedushat Ha-aretz 2:7.<br>At the time when purity was practiced, it was a mitzva to bring to the kohen even terumah or ḥalla that had become impure, and the kohen could use them as fuel for his oven. However, today, when the possibility of eating them has ceased, the custom is not to give impure terumah and ḥalla to a kohen lest people come to eat it; rather, the one who separates ḥalla burns it, or wraps it and disposes of it in the garbage bin (Radbaz 1:731; see above 7:7; 9:6)."}]},{"slug":"17-11-05","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/17-11-05/","title_he":"ה – סדר הפרשת חלה למעשה","title_en":"The Order of Separating Ḥalla in Practice","body_en":"<p>It is a mitzva to separate ḥalla from dough intended for baking whose flour is from the five species of grain (below halakha 8), and which contains a significant measure, from which food sufficient for at least an entire day can be prepared, that is, whose volume of flour is at least the volume of 43.2 eggs, about 2.160 liters (below halakha 6).</p><p>The mitzva applies to men and women alike, but if the husband and wife knead the dough together, she has precedence in the mitzva, because she is more responsible for household matters (see Or Zaru'a, end of 225; Rashi, Shabbat 32a).</p><p>One who separates ḥalla from the flour has not fulfilled the mitzva, for only after the flour and water are mixed does the obligation of the mitzva begin and it is possible to separate ḥalla, and the choicest time is at the completion of kneading the dough (SA 327:3).</p><p>Before fulfilling the mitzva one recites the blessing: <b>\"Blessed are You, Lord our God, King of the universe, who has sanctified us with His commandments and commanded us to separate ḥalla\"</b> (those of Yemenite descent and part of Ashkenaz), or \"to separate ḥalla as terumah\" (Eastern communities), or \"to separate ḥalla from the dough\" (many of Ashkenazic and North African descent).<sup>3</sup></p><p>After the blessing, one separates a piece of any size, and some say at the time of separation: \"Behold, this is ḥalla.\" Since it is forbidden to eat the ḥalla, it must be burned, so that people will not stumble by eating it. One may also place it in the garbage bin, and so that it will not be treated with disrespect, one should first wrap it and then place it in the garbage bin. And so many people do (above 9:5, note 8).<sup>4</sup></p><p>If they forgot to separate ḥalla from the dough, they separate it after baking, and thereby permit the baked item. One who is concerned that perhaps ḥalla was not separated from a baked item that was served to him should separate a small crumb from it as ḥalla, and place it in a place where there is no concern that people will come to eat it.</p><p>From the time that girls and boys reach the age of mitzvot, they may fulfill the mitzva.<sup>5</sup></p><p>A person may appoint a Jew as an agent to separate ḥalla from his dough on his behalf, and then the agent separates with a blessing. One may also appoint the cook as a permanent agent, to separate ḥalla from all the doughs he kneads throughout all the years of his work. But one who separates ḥalla contrary to the wishes of the owner of the dough – his separation is not effective (SA 328:3).</p>","notes_en":[{"n":3,"en":"There are many customs regarding the wording of the blessing, and all are valid: \"to separate ḥalla\" (Rambam 5:11; Kitzur Shulḥan Arukh 35:1; Arukh Ha-shulḥan 328:2; Olat Re'iya p. 353; Shemirat Shabbat Ke-hilkhata 42:12; Derekh Emuna 5:100); \"to separate ḥalla as terumah\" (Ben Ish Ḥai; Kaf Ha-ḥayim 457:9); \"to separate ḥalla from the dough\" (Rashba; Siddur Beit Ya'akov); \"to separate terumah\" (SA 328:1; Gra 2); \"to separate terumah, ḥalla\" (Maharshal, Ḥatam Sofer). The root of the difference in customs is that in the language of the Torah, ḥalla is a dough, and from it one separates \"terumah,\" as it is stated (Bamidbar 15:20): \"Of the first of your dough you shall set apart a cake (ḥalla) as a gift (terumah).\" And in the language of the Sages, the mitzva and what is given to the kohen are called \"ḥalla,\" and even the tractate that deals with the mitzva is called \"Ḥalla.\""},{"n":4,"en":"The Rambam and the SA did not write that one must say \"Behold, this is ḥalla.\" And some hold that one must say it (based on the Ra'avad). Many women do so, but it is not obligatory.<br>As we learned above 9, note 8, according to many Rishonim it is a mitzva to burn impure terumah and ḥalla, and some ruled so in practice (Sha'arei Tzedek 14:34; Ha-ma'aser Ve-ha-teruma 10, se'if katan 30; Tevu'at Ha-sadeh 9:10). When one burns the ḥalla, one should not place it on a baking tray, because the ḥalla is forbidden to be eaten and its taste is absorbed into the tray; rather, one should place it on the floor of the oven, and one may do this even while baking another food in the oven, since there is no possibility that the taste of the ḥalla will enter the food. And some are stringent to wrap it in aluminum foil (see Tevu'at Ha-sadeh 8:6). In practice, many have the custom to be lenient to wrap it and place it in the garbage bin. And as we learned there, since burning involves difficulty, and waiting until the burning may cause mishaps, the custom is to be lenient. And some say that when one separates less than a kezayit for ḥalla, according to all opinions one need not burn it (Yehuda Ya'aleh YD 352). However, the Rema (322:5) wrote that the custom is to beautify the mitzva by separating the measure of a kezayit. And the one who chooses will choose in what to beautify the mitzva.<br>When the kohanim used to eat the ḥalla, people were very careful that it not become impure, and therefore they hurried to separate ḥalla at the first possible time, that is, when the bulk of the flour had become mixed with water. However, today, when we are all impure, one waits until the choicest time, which is when the mixture becomes a cohesive dough (SA 327:2-3)."},{"n":5,"en":"Girls and boys who separated ḥalla in the year adjacent to their reaching the age of mitzvot – according to the Rambam, after the fact they have fulfilled the mitzva, like the law of the vow of a \"mufla ha-samukh le-ish\" (a precocious minor close to adulthood), provided that they understood what they did (Rambam, Hilkhot Terumot 4:5; SA 331:33). And some say that as long as they have not reached the age of mitzvot, they do not have the power to separate (Bi'ur Ha-Gra 331:78, in accordance with the anonymous Mishna in Terumot 1:1)."}]},{"slug":"17-11-06","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/17-11-06/","title_he":"ו – שיעור החיוב בחלה","title_en":"The Measure That Obligates Ḥalla","body_en":"<p>The dough that is obligated in ḥalla is the dough that people used to prepare in the generation of the wilderness, as it is stated (Bamidbar 15:20): \"Of the first of your dough (arisoteikhem) you shall set apart a cake (ḥalla) as a gift.\" \"Arisa\" is the dough that they would prepare from the manna that fell in the wilderness each day for each person, as it is stated (Shemot 16:16): \"This is the thing that the Lord has commanded: Gather of it every man according to his eating, an omer a head, according to the number of your persons, shall you take it, every man for those who are in his tent.\" They would grind the manna and knead it into dough (Bamidbar 11:8), and its measure for each person was a tenth of an eifa, as it is stated (Shemot 16:36): \"Now an omer is the tenth part of an eifa\" (Eruvin 83b). But dough whose measure is less than this has no significance and is exempt from ḥalla.</p><p>A tenth of an eifa is a volume of forty-three and a fifth eggs – 2.160 liters (an egg being about 50 cc), and le-khatḥila it is good to round the calculation up to 2.200 liters. The calculation is of the flour before it is kneaded with water, and without additions of salt and spices. In order to be precise in the measure of the flour, one should measure it in vessels that have volume markings. When it is not possible to measure the volume, one makes use of calculating the measure by weight; however, since this is an estimate that depends on the degree of compression of the flour, there is doubt in it, for sometimes flour of the volume that obligates ḥalla weighs more and sometimes less. Therefore, it is necessary to establish two measures: one for the obligation to separate out of doubt without a blessing, and the second for the obligation to separate with a blessing. And since there are differences between types of flour, it is necessary to establish a measure for each flour, as explained in the table.<sup>6</sup></p><p><b>Table (weights in kilograms):</b><br>Whole wheat – with a blessing (compressed flour): 1.650; without a blessing (loose flour): 1.450.<br>White wheat and spelt – with a blessing (compressed flour): 1.550; without a blessing (loose flour): 1.250.<br>Rye – with a blessing (compressed flour): 1.350; without a blessing (loose flour): 1.150.<br>Oats and barley – with a blessing (compressed flour): 1.200; without a blessing (loose flour): 1.050.</p>","notes_en":[{"n":6,"en":"The measure of ḥalla is 43 eggs plus a fifth of an egg (Rif; Rambam, Hilkhot Bikkurim 6:15; Rosh; SA 324:1). This measure is in volume of flour. As is well known, with water the measures of volume and weight are identical, but wheat flour is lighter than water of the same volume by about a third to about a half. The difference depends on the density of the flour and its type. Therefore, the precise calculation is by volume. The Kol Bo (48:9) was in doubt whether one calculates the volume of the flour aerated or compressed. However, from all the poskim, Rishonim and Aḥaronim, who were not in doubt about this, it emerges explicitly that one measures the flour according to its volume as it is before us. So wrote explicitly Tosafot, Menaḥot 52b, s.v. \"ḥasera\"; Meiri, Pesaḥim 48b; Magen Avraham 456:4; Pri Ḥadash 1; Maḥatzit Ha-shekel. Therefore, best of all is to prepare a vessel of the volume measure of ḥalla, in order to measure the flour in it as it is, and whatever contains a volume of 2.160 liters, one should separate from it with a blessing, and le-khatḥila it is good that it be 2.200 liters.<br>However, often there are no vessels for calculating volume, but there is a scale, or the flour comes in packages by weight; for this purpose the poskim estimated its weight. But when one calculates its weight, the doubt returns, because when the volume measure of ḥalla is compressed, its weight (for white wheat flour) is about 1.550 kg, and when it is aerated – 1.250 kg. And since we do not know how to determine the state of the flour, whether aerated or compressed, it is necessary to establish two measures in the table above: if it reached the lower one, one must separate ḥalla without a blessing, and if it reached the higher one, one separates with a blessing.<br><b>Expansion on calculating the measure of ḥalla</b>: According to the Rambam, if we compare an identical volume of flour and water, the flour will be lighter by about a third (Commentary on the Mishna, Eduyot 1:2), and the weight of flour obligated in ḥalla is about 520 Egyptian zuz (dirhams) (Hilkhot Bikkurim 6:15). On this basis Rabbi Ḥayim Na'eh calculated that the measure obligated in ḥalla is 1.666 kilograms (the volume of an egg – 57.6 cc, multiplied by 43.2, is 2.488 liters). However, an error crept into his calculation, for it was made according to the Turkish dirham of his day, whose weight is 3.205 grams, and about four hundred years ago the Turks added about 12% to its weight, whereas the dirham that existed in the time of the Rambam weighed only 2.83 grams (Peninei Halakha: Berakhot 10:10, note 11). Therefore, in practice the measure of an egg is 50 cc, and the volume measure obligated in ḥalla is 2.160 liters. [Indeed, according to a precise measure of ḥalla according to the Rambam, by 520 dirhams multiplied by 2.83 grams, it comes out that an egg is 50.94 cc, and the measure of ḥalla 2.200. However, on the other hand, dirham coins weighing 2.81 grams have been found, and accordingly an egg is 50.58, and the volume measure of ḥalla 2.185 liters. And according to the early Ashkenazic custom, the measure of an egg is about 46 cc, and accordingly the volume measure of ḥalla is 1.987 liters (Middot Ve-shiurei Torah by Rav Benish 13:4-5). And the Ashkenazic measure corresponds to the Roman and Egyptian cubits. Also according to the Rambam, Eduyot 1:2, the true measure is slightly less. In summary, since the measures of the Sages generally tend to be round numbers, one should establish that an egg is 50 cc, and accordingly one must separate ḥalla with a blessing from a measure of 2.160, and for ease of calculation with vessels not designed to measure 2.160, it is good to round up to 2.200.]<br>A further doubt arose, for when Rabbi Ḥayim Na'eh measured the volume of flour against its weight, he found that sometimes the weight measure is about half of the volume measure, and not two-thirds; therefore he ruled to separate ḥalla without a blessing already from a weight of 1,250 grams of wheat flour. In practice, according to calculations we made, when the flour is compressed its weight is slightly more than two-thirds, and with whole wheat flour much more than two-thirds, and this is how it is given in the table above regarding separation with a blessing. And perhaps as a result of storage in packed bags, the compression today is greater. In any case, in order to resolve the doubt, the custom today is to give two weight measures for the mitzva of ḥalla: one for separating ḥalla out of doubt without a blessing, and the second for the measure of separating ḥalla with a blessing. One could indeed argue that when there is doubt there is no obligation to separate ḥalla, as we learned that since today the law of ḥalla is rabbinic, one is lenient in cases of doubt (SA 325:1 and Shakh 2; SA 330:4 and Shakh 8; and see Mishpetei Eretz 2, note 13). However, one can say that whenever it is a perpetual doubt, one should be stringent, as in the case of the udder and the asham [?]. In any case, to be on the safe side, the custom is to be stringent and separate without a blessing from a measure regarding which there is doubt whether it perhaps contains the volume measure of ḥalla (see Mishpetei Eretz 2, note 2, and the table on p. 477, and Tevu'ot Ha-sadeh p. 147). In our table the calculation is according to an egg volume of 50 cc, and regarding the measure for separation with a blessing we rounded upward in order to resolve the doubt.<br>One who separated without a blessing from a doubtful measure, and afterward added another amount to it so that it is now obligated in ḥalla without doubt, must separate again without a blessing.<br>According to the Tzelaḥ and the Ḥazon Ish, eggs in the past were double those of our day, and their volume close to 100 cc, and the measure of ḥalla 4.320 liters; however, according to the measurements of the Ḥazon Ish, the weight of wheat flour is slightly more than half its volume and not two-thirds, and therefore one must separate with a blessing from flour weighing 2.250 kilograms (see Mishpetei Eretz 2:1). (Similarly, the Ben Ish Ḥai, Tzav 19, calculates weight as volume, and it comes out close to the measure of the Ḥazon Ish; and see Peninei Halakha: Berakhot 10:6, note 7.) However, in practice the custom is not to take into account their approach, against which there are serious difficulties (and see Tevu'at Ha-sadeh 4:1, that Rav Shlomo Zalman Auerbach and Rav Yosef Shalom Elyashiv wrote that the custom follows Rabbi Ḥayim Na'eh).<br>Some have doubts regarding whole wheat flour, that perhaps they separated the bran (the husk of the kernel) and returned it to the flour, and perhaps in such a case it does not combine toward the measure and must be deducted from the measure, for it is explained in Mishna Ḥalla 2:6: \"Five quarter-kav of flour – they are obligated in ḥalla, they, their leaven, their fine bran and their coarse bran … if their coarse bran was removed from them and returned into them – they are exempt.\" (So wrote Mishpetei Eretz 2, note 2. And according to Tevu'at Ha-sadeh 4:5, because of the doubt one should add 15% to the measure for a blessing.) It does not seem that one need be concerned about this, because it is explained in the Gemara, Shabbat 76b, and in the commentators on the Mishna, that the bran that was returned does not combine because people do not usually prepare dough this way, except for the poor. However, today, when the bran is considered to improve the dough, this is how people usually prepare dough, and it is obvious that the bran combines. So too wrote Rav Machta in Emunat Itekha 112.<br>And one may ask: the measure of ḥalla is the measure of the manna, that is, the measure that an average person eats in a day. And if we assume that its caloric value is like that of wheat or barley (one hundred grams about 330 calories), it turns out that a person would eat close to 5,000 calories a day, whereas an ordinary person in our day needs to eat about 2,000 to 2,500. And sometimes they would also eat meat in the evening. And see Mishna Ketubot 5:8, that the flour sufficient for a poor woman for a week is two kav of wheat (whereas the measure of ḥalla, which is the volume measure of daily eating, is a kav and three-quarters). Likewise, we learned regarding eating a peras that the volume of bread an average person would eat in a day is about eight eggs (Eruvin 82b; Peninei Halakha: Berakhot 10:7, note 8). Indeed, they ate other foods with the bread, but bread was the main food. Therefore, one must say that the caloric value of the manna was significantly lower than that of grain, and each person received from it according to the measure he needed. Indeed, in terms of volume, the measure of ḥalla resembles the volume of foods that a person usually eats in a day."}]},{"slug":"17-11-07","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/17-11-07/","title_he":"ז – צירופי בצק ומאפים","title_en":"Combining Doughs and Baked Goods","body_en":"<p>Doughs or baked goods, none of which individually contains the measure of ḥalla, that were combined together into the measure – one must separate ḥalla from them with a blessing. The combination is created in two ways: 1) When the two doughs stuck to each other during kneading or baking, such that if they were separated they would \"bite\" each other, that is, a little of the dough or baked item of one would be torn off onto the other. 2) When the doughs or baked goods are placed in one vessel or one bag, or when they are covered with a cloth – they combine to be considered one bread (SA 325:1). If the baked goods or doughs were placed in a refrigerator, a freezer, an oven, or a cabinet fixed in the wall, there is a doubt whether they combine – and therefore one should separate ḥalla from them without a blessing.<sup>7</sup></p><p>One who does not wish to bake bread in the measure that obligates ḥalla need not bake more than his needs in order to fulfill the mitzva. But one who intends to bake the measure that obligates ḥalla may not divide it in two in order to exempt himself from the mitzva (Yerushalmi Ḥalla 3:1; SA 324:14). And even though the mitzva today is rabbinic, and the kohanim cannot eat the ḥalla because of impurity, it is forbidden to evade the mitzva, for through its fulfillment we connect to the idea hidden within it.</p><p>Two doughs or two baked items, each of which contains the measure of ḥalla – one may separate from one on behalf of the other as long as they are in one place, in the category of \"mukaf\" (as explained above 9:3). After the fact, if one separated not from the mukaf – he has fulfilled his obligation; and so the custom is le-khatḥila when people forgot to separate ḥalla and have already distributed the baked goods to other people, that one separates ḥalla from one baked item on behalf of baked goods that are in another place (SA 325:2; 331:25; above 9:3).</p>","notes_en":[{"n":7,"en":"In Mishna Ḥalla 2:4: \"One who makes his dough in kav-sized portions and they touched each other – they are exempt from ḥalla until they 'bite' [each other]. Rabbi Eliezer says: Even one who removes [loaves from the oven] and places them in a basket – the basket combines them for ḥalla.\" And in Pesaḥim 48b it is explained that the halakha follows Rabbi Eliezer, who does not disagree with the Tanna Kamma. And so it was ruled in the Rambam and SA 325:1.<br>Based on the Gemara, Pesaḥim 48b, the Rishonim disagreed whether an oven combines: according to the Rambam and those who agree with him, it does not combine, and according to the Rosh and those who agree with him, it combines. And since many hold like the Rosh, even though ḥalla in our times is rabbinic, one should combine them again in a basket and separate with a blessing. And when it is not possible to combine them, one should separate without a blessing. The same law applies to combination by means of a refrigerator, freezer and wall cabinet (Tevu'at Ha-sadeh 3:28; Shemirat Shabbat Ke-hilkhata 42, note 39; Mishpetei Eretz 2:16; Kedushat Ha-aretz 5:7). A table – it is doubtful whether it combines, since it does not have walls like a vessel (Pesaḥim 48b), and the poskim wrote that since ḥalla in our times is rabbinic, it does not combine (Rambam and SA 325:1; Shakh 2). Some say that baked goods combine only when they are wrapped in a cloth on all sides (Agudah and Maharil), and according to the halakha, even if they are covered with a cloth from above, they combine to separate with a blessing (Ḥokhmat Adam, Arukh Ha-shulḥan, Bi'ur Halakha 457:1, s.v. \"ve-ha-sal\"). Baked goods, part of which are within the space of the vessel and part in a pile above the space of the vessel – according to the Tur they do not combine, and according to the Semag in the name of Ri they combine; one should separate without a blessing. And if he covers them with a cloth, he should separate with a blessing (Kaf Ha-ḥayim 457:28). Some say that the combination is effective only immediately after they are taken out of the oven (Maharam Ḥalawa and Nimukei Yosef), and according to the halakha they combine even after some time, or while they are still doughs (Mikdash Me'at 325:10; Rav Pe'alim vol. 4, YD 36; Mishpetei Eretz 2:13). Some say that with combination by a basket they must also touch each other (Semag and Rosh), and in practice they combine even without contact (Rambam; SA 326:1; Pri Ḥadash; Rabbi Ḥayim Palagi; Rav Shlomo Zalman Auerbach; Kedushat Ha-aretz 5:3; and see Mishpetei Eretz 2, note 30). In all the cases of doubt in which I wrote to recite a blessing, I wrote according to the custom. Indeed, in some books of our time they wrote not to recite a blessing out of concern for doubt regarding blessings. However, since most of the Aḥaronim knew that there are those who disagree and nevertheless ruled to separate with a blessing, this is the custom, and in a matter of custom one is not concerned about doubt regarding blessings. (One can further say that perhaps all the discussions and disputes are when there was no special intention to combine, but when one wishes to combine, and there is an aspect of combination in the state of the doughs or the baked goods, everyone can agree that it combines.) I did not write the opinion of the stringent above, because one should not burden the learners by writing all the opinions, and I wrote them here in the note so that those who are meticulous will know that it is good to take them into account le-khatḥila.<br>One who separated ḥalla from a dough that did not contain the measure of obligation has done nothing, and there is no sanctity in the ḥalla he separated. Therefore, if they separated ḥalla from two doughs that did not contain the measure of obligation, and afterward they were combined together, one must separate ḥalla from them with a blessing (Mishna Ḥalla 4:5; SA 326:5). With baked goods made from different species of grain, sometimes there is combination and sometimes not, as explained in SA 324:2 (but if different flours of the species of grain were mixed – they combine, as explained in halakha 9). One does not separate from dough made from flour of one year on behalf of dough from flour of another year, and if one separated, he has done nothing, for so the Sages decreed, so that people would not mistakenly think that it is also permitted to separate terumot and ma'asrot from one year on behalf of another. But on the other hand, if they combined dough less than the measure from one year with less than the measure from another year, their combination obligates them, and one must make additional doughs from both years in order to separate from them on behalf of the dough in which flour from two years is mixed (SA 324:8; Shakh 16; above 8:5)."}]},{"slug":"17-11-08","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/17-11-08/","title_he":"ח – עיסה שנועדה לחלוקה – פטורה","title_en":"Dough Intended to Be Divided Is Exempt","body_en":"<p>Two different doughs that one is particular not to mix, such as yeast dough and short-crust dough, do not combine. Moreover, even one dough that is going to be divided into two parts, half for a sweet baked item and half for a savory baked item – even though it is now one dough, the parts that are going to be divided do not combine (SA 326:1).</p><p>Women who kneaded dough together so that each of them would take her portion for herself – even though the shared dough contains the measure of ḥalla, since they are going to divide it, it does not combine into the measure. And even if they bake everything in one oven, as long as each one recognizes her baked item and is particular to take it for herself, the baked goods do not combine into the measure.</p><p>Therefore, women who wish to bake breads or cakes together in order to merit the mitzva, if none of them has the measure of ḥalla on her own, must be careful to be full partners in all the baked goods until the completion of baking, and after the baking each one should take her portion for herself at random, that is, without there being a marking that this is her baked item; in this way one of them can separate ḥalla on behalf of all of them. But if each one braids dough for herself in a special form and is particular to take it afterward, since they divided the doughs before baking, their doughs do not combine. Likewise, a kindergarten teacher who bakes ḥallot with the children in preparation for Shabbat, where each child kneads his own dough and his name is stuck on it – their ḥallot do not combine.<sup>8</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":8,"en":"Mishna Ḥalla 4:1: \"Two women who made two kav [of dough] and they touched each other – even if they are of one species, they are exempt. And when they belong to one woman – a species with its own species is obligated, and not with its own species is exempt.\" Yerushalmi there: \"Rabbi Yoḥanan said: An ordinary single woman is not particular; two are particular. A single woman who is particular (for example, she has two types of dough that she does not want to be mixed) – they treated her like two women. Two women who are not particular – they treated them like one woman.\"<br>When one kneads dough of the measure and freezes part of it for the following Shabbat, one must separate ḥalla with a blessing. Indeed, some are concerned that dividing the dough for baking on different days divides the measure, and they separate without a blessing (Leket Ha-omer 7, note 9). However, the custom is to separate with a blessing (Mishpetei Eretz 2, note 46; Tevu'at Ha-sadeh 4, note 7). And so it seems, because when one freezes, it is clear that if there is a need, they will take it out of the freezer and bake it, and thus perhaps they will come to be combined, and their law is like that of a baker, as explained in SA 326:2. And one cannot argue that when dividing it for different days this is not the normal manner of kneading, because today it is possible to freeze, and consequently it is accepted to knead the measure and bake part and freeze part; and since the obligation is at the time of kneading – one must separate with a blessing.<br>Sometimes a party is held, and the neighbors and relatives bring various baked goods that they prepared themselves, and they did not separate ḥalla from them because they did not contain the measure. If all the baked goods were gathered into a basket or a box, and there are baked goods there in the measure obligating ḥalla from one type of dough that people are not particular about keeping from mixing – such as from ordinary yeast dough for bread, ḥallot and rolls, or baked goods from sweet yeast dough for cakes, or from short-crust dough – since they were placed in one place for the purpose of the party, they combine, and one must separate ḥalla from them. But baked goods from different doughs that people are particular not to mix at the time of kneading do not combine. Baked goods from one type of dough, some of which are filled with chocolate and some with jam – since the dough is of one type – they combine. But dairy baked goods with meat ones, or even with pareve ones, do not combine, since people are particular to divide them before baking (Kedushat Ha-aretz 5:11-14).<br>When the measure was kneaded in order to be divided, and therefore it was exempt from ḥalla, even if dough was afterward added in order to reach the measure of obligation – the part that was kneaded first was entirely removed from the mitzva, since it already had a time when it contained the quantity of the measure of obligation and was exempted (Rambam and SA 326:4)."}]},{"slug":"17-11-09","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/17-11-09/","title_he":"ט – הקמח והנוזלים","title_en":"The Flour and the Liquids","body_en":"<p>The mitzva is to separate ḥalla from dough intended for baking bread, as it is stated (Bamidbar 15:19): \"then it shall be that when you eat of the bread of the land.\" Therefore, the dough obligated in the mitzva is dough made from one of the five species of grain, which are: wheat, barley, spelt, oats and rye. Their uniqueness is that they can become leavened, and therefore one can make bread and cakes from their dough, and when one is careful that they not become leavened, one can make matzot for the mitzva on Pesaḥ from them. But one who makes a baked item from corn flour or rice flour is not obligated in ḥalla, since their dough cannot become leavened and is not called bread, and therefore it is also impossible to make matzot for Pesaḥ from them (Peninei Halakha: Pesaḥ 2:3). It is worth noting that there is a species of legume called kusemet (buckwheat), and even if flour is made from it, it is not obligated in ḥalla, since it is not a species of grain.</p><p>One who mixed flours of the five species of grain and made from them one dough containing the measure of ḥalla – it is obligated in ḥalla (SA 324:2). One who mixed flours of the five species of grain with other ingredients, such as cornflour, sugar, cocoa and eggs – if three conditions are met in the mixture, one separates ḥalla with a blessing:<br>1) the baked item has the taste of grain; 2) the flour of the species of grain contains the measure of ḥalla;<br>3) the species of grain are the majority – one separates ḥalla with a blessing. And if the third condition is not met, that is, the baked item has the taste of grain and the measure of ḥalla, but the species of grain are not the majority – one separates ḥalla without a blessing. And if one of the first two conditions is not met, that is, the baked item does not have the taste of grain, or the grain does not contain the measure of ḥalla – it is exempt from ḥalla.<sup>9</sup></p><p>One who kneads the flour with significant liquids is obligated in ḥalla. Six of the seven significant liquids are permitted for eating, and they are: water, dew, olive oil, wine, milk and bees' honey (the seventh, blood, is forbidden to be eaten). But if one kneaded the flour with other liquids, such as fruit juice or egg liquid, there is a doubt whether the dough is obligated in ḥalla, and one should separate ḥalla without a blessing. However, if a little water is added to the fruit juice, one must separate ḥalla with a blessing.<sup>10</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":9,"en":"Mishna Ḥalla 3:7: \"One who makes dough from wheat and from rice – if it has the taste of grain, it is obligated in ḥalla, and a person fulfills his obligation with it on Pesaḥ; and if it does not have the taste of grain, it is not obligated in ḥalla, and a person does not fulfill his obligation with it on Pesaḥ.\" According to the Rambam (Hilkhot Bikkurim 6:11) and SA (324:9-10), with wheat and rice flour there is a unique law, according to which even when the wheat flour is a minority, whenever the mixture has the taste of grain, even if it does not contain the measure of ḥalla – since the nature of rice is that it is drawn after the wheat – if the mixture as a whole contains the measure, one must separate ḥalla (according to the Rambam the wheat need not even contain a kezayit within the time of eating a peras, and according to the Ra'avad it must). And they learned from this that if species of grain were mixed with other species, according to the Rambam, if the grain contains the measure of ḥalla, even if it is not the majority, as long as it contains a kezayit within the time of eating a peras – one must separate ḥalla; and according to the Ra'avad the grain must also be the majority (Tevu'at Ha-sadeh 3, note 4; and see MB 453:14). And according to most of the Aḥaronim, according to the Rosh, even if the grain does not contain the measure of ḥalla, as long as the taste of the baked item is the taste of grain and the mixture as a whole contains the measure, it is obligated in ḥalla (provided that there is a kezayit within the time of eating a peras – Magen Avraham; and some say even if there is not a kezayit within the time of eating a peras). And some say that the opinion of the Rosh is like that of the Rambam (Beit Efrayim, Avnei Nezer).<br>And many Rishonim (Ramban, Rashba, Or Zaru'a and others) ruled in accordance with the opinion of Rabban Shimon ben Gamliel cited in the Yerushalmi 3:5: \"It is never obligated in ḥalla unless it contains grain in the [required] measure.\" And according to them, even with wheat and rice flour, whenever the wheat flour does not contain the measure, it is exempt from ḥalla. And if it contains the measure of ḥalla, even if it is not the majority, it is obligated in ḥalla. And with other species, only if it contains the measure and the grain is the majority – it is obligated in ḥalla.<br>In practice, regarding all the mixtures (except for wheat and rice), as long as the grain does not contain the measure, it is exempt from ḥalla (and the meticulous separate without a blessing in accordance with the opinion of the Rosh; see Mishpetei Eretz 1:14-16). And when the grain contains the measure but is not the majority, one separates ḥalla without a blessing. And with wheat and rice, whenever the baked item has the taste of grain, one must separate ḥalla without a blessing, and when the wheat contains the measure, one separates with a blessing."},{"n":10,"en":"Mishna Ḥalla 2:2: \"Dough that was kneaded with fruit juice is obligated in ḥalla.\" According to most of the Rishonim, this is the law with all liquids (Ramban, Rashba, Rashbatz, and many in explaining the opinion of the Rambam). And according to the Rosh, only if it was kneaded with the significant \"seven liquids\" is it obligated in ḥalla. Seemingly, from SA 329:9 it is implied, like most of the Rishonim, that what is kneaded with any type of fruit juice is obligated in ḥalla; however, in OḤ 462:5 he wrote not to knead the measure, and some explain that he was concerned for the opinion of the Rosh, that it is exempt from ḥalla (MB 462:20; Sha'ar Ha-tziyun 27). In practice, one takes into account the opinion of the Rosh, and whatever was kneaded with liquids that are not significant, one separates ḥalla from it without a blessing (Baḥ, Shakh, Panim Me'irot, Arukh Ha-shulḥan, Rav Ovadya Yosef, Or Le-Tziyon). And some explain that according to the Rosh, only what is kneaded with water is obligated in ḥalla (Beit Hillel YD 329:1). And some were concerned about this (Ben Ish Ḥai). But since it is a minority opinion in explaining a minority opinion, the custom is not to be concerned about it and to separate with a blessing. When a little water is added to fruit juice that is not one of the seven liquids, one must separate with a blessing. And if a little of the seven liquids other than water was added to it, one should separate without a blessing; and if they are the majority, one should separate with a blessing (Sha'ar Ha-melekh, Hilkhot Berakhot 6:1; Rabbi Akiva Eiger, and many others).<br>If the flour was kneaded with fruit juice, since it has not yet become wet with one of the seven significant liquids, it has not yet been rendered susceptible to receiving impurity from the hands at the time of kneading. Consequently, the ḥalla that is separated is pure, and it is forbidden to render it impure or to destroy it by burning or in another way; rather, one must place it in the garbage bin inside a nylon bag, in such a manner that it will rot by itself without a person causing it. In practice, most wheat today is washed with water, so that the flour has already been rendered susceptible to impurity before kneading, and consequently one may burn the ḥalla. However, when there is a doubt whether it was washed, one should be stringent not to burn the ḥalla."}]},{"slug":"17-11-10","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/17-11-10/","title_he":"י – בצק של מאפה – לחם","title_en":"Dough for Baking – Bread","body_en":"<p>As we have learned, the mitzva is to separate ḥalla from dough intended for baking bread, as it is stated (Bamidbar 15:19-21): \"then it shall be that when you eat of the bread of the land … of the first of your dough (your dough) you shall give to the Lord a gift throughout your generations.\" Included in \"bread\" are all grain baked goods, such as cakes, crackers, biscuits, baked goods filled with meat, cheese or vegetables, bagels, pretzel sticks and baklava. However, over bread people customarily establish a meal, and therefore over it one always recites \"Ha-motzi\" and Birkat Ha-mazon, whereas the other baked goods are intended for casual eating, and therefore only when one establishes a meal over them does one recite \"Ha-motzi\" and Birkat Ha-mazon, and when one eats them in their usual manner, casually, one recites over them \"Mezonot\" and \"Al Ha-miḥya.\" By contrast, over cooked grain dishes, such as ptitim (toasted pasta pellets) and noodles, even if one establishes a meal, one always recites \"Mezonot\" and \"Al Ha-miḥya,\" for since they are a cooked dish and not a baked item, they are not bread. This is the rule: a grain baked item is called bread, and it is a mitzva to separate ḥalla from it; and a cooked grain dish is not bread, and there is no mitzva to separate ḥalla from it (this is the opinion of most poskim, as explained in the next halakha).</p><p>Cooking and baking are both done with heat, but cooking is done within boiling liquids, whereas a baked item, although it contains liquids, thanks to which it is baked and not burned, is not placed within liquids while it is being baked. Generally baking is done in an oven, but one can also prepare baked goods in a frying pan or a pot placed on a fire. And even though people customarily put a little oil on their bottom so that the dough will not stick, or in order to add flavor to it, since the liquids around the dough are minimal, they are considered baked goods. Therefore, one must separate ḥalla with a blessing from baked goods such as malawaḥ and mufleta, which are baked in a frying pan, and kubaneh and jaḥnun, which are baked in a pot; and if one establishes a meal over them, one recites \"Ha-motzi\" and Birkat Ha-mazon.<sup>11</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":11,"en":"It is explained in the Bavli, Pesaḥim 37a, and the Yerushalmi, Ḥalla 1:3, that according to Reish Lakish a baked item is only what is made in an oven or in a pan that was heated before the dough was placed in it. And according to Rabbi Yoḥanan, any dough baked in a pan or pot without much liquid is a baked item. According to Rabbeinu Ḥananel the halakha follows Reish Lakish, and according to the overwhelming majority of the Rishonim the halakha follows Rabbi Yoḥanan, and this is the opinion of the She'iltot, Halakhot Gedolot, Rambam, Ri, Rosh, Rashba, SA 329:2.<br>Some were in doubt regarding kubaneh and jaḥnun when they are baked in a closed pot with much moisture (Shulḥan Arukh Ha-mekutzar YD 170:18). And some were in doubt also regarding malawaḥ and mufleta, and therefore ruled to separate from them without a blessing (Hilkhot Ha-aretz 12, note 3). However, one should not multiply doubts needlessly, and since the dough of all these baked goods is thick, even though much liquid was mixed into it, as long as it does not pour like a liquid, and it is not placed within much liquid, its law is like that of a baked item. Therefore, all these baked goods are obligated in ḥalla, and one who establishes a meal over them recites \"Ha-motzi\" and Birkat Ha-mazon.<br>I wrote that even if one put a little oil in the pot or pan for flavoring, it is still considered a baked item, and with this many doubts are dismissed. Indeed, some understood that only when the purpose of the liquids is to prevent sticking is it a baked item (see Rema OḤ 168:14); however, it is reasonable that only when a significant part of the dough is immersed in liquids is it considered cooked, and so it is implied by the Meiri, Pesaḥim 37a, and the Rash, Ḥalla 1:4. So too wrote Mikdash Me'at 329:2."}]},{"slug":"17-11-11","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/17-11-11/","title_he":"יא – בצק עבה שנועד לבישול","title_en":"Thick Dough Intended for Cooking","body_en":"<p>When one intends to make from the dough sufganiyot (doughnuts), noodles, ptitim, dumplings (kreplach) or soup croutons (shkedei marak) – since one intends to prepare them for eating by cooking in water or frying in deep oil, they are exempt from ḥalla. And some say that all these doughs are obligated in ḥalla, because the time of the obligation of ḥalla is at the kneading of the dough, and since their dough is thick and fit for baking as bread, even though the intention is to cook them, one must separate ḥalla from them (Rabbeinu Tam). In practice, the halakha follows the opinion of most poskim, that since one intends to cook these doughs, they are not considered bread, and even if one establishes a meal over them, their blessing is \"Mezonot\" and \"Al Ha-miḥya.\" However, le-khatḥila, in order to satisfy all opinions, the custom is to separate ḥalla from them without a blessing.<sup>12</sup></p><p>If one bakes part of the dough that one intends to cook, even the size of a small cookie, according to all opinions one will be obligated to separate ḥalla with a blessing from that baked item. And this is the advice for one who wishes to resolve the doubt and merit the mitzva: to decide in advance to bake a small cookie from the dough in the oven or in a frying pan (Maharam of Rothenburg; SA 329:4).<sup>13</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":12,"en":"According to Rabbeinu Tam, since the time of the obligation of ḥalla is at the kneading, any dough from which it is fit to make bread is obligated in ḥalla, and all that Rabbi Yoḥanan and Reish Lakish disagreed about regarding a pan-made item (ma'aseh ilpas) is a soft batter that was poured into a pan or pot and baked, such as a stirred cake (uga beḥusha); but if the dough was thick, even if it was afterward cooked, it is obligated in ḥalla. This is the opinion of the Roke'aḥ, Yere'im, Rabbeinu Peretz, Kol Bo and Ha-hashlama. However, there is a dispute about the opinion of Rabbeinu Tam: some say that only if the dough will in the end look similar to bread, such as sufganiyot and fricassee, is it obligated in ḥalla according to Rabbeinu Tam, but noodles and the like, since their form is not the form of bread – even according to Rabbeinu Tam they are exempt (Talmidei Rabbeinu Yona, Maharit Algazi). And some say that even noodle dough is obligated in ḥalla, since the dough was thick (Mordekhai, Or Zaru'a, Panim Me'irot).<br>However, according to the overwhelming majority of the Rishonim, only dough intended for baking is considered bread and obligated in ḥalla, but if it is intended for cooking or deep-frying in oil, since it will not be bread, it is exempt from ḥalla (Rash, Rid, Ramban, Rambam, Ra'avya, Rashba, Meiri, Maharam Ḥalawa, Rabbeinu Yeruḥam, Ḥinukh). And so ruled SA 329:3, the Gra and Shulḥan Arukh Ha-Rav. However, the Shakh 329:4 wrote that one should take into account the opinion of Rabbeinu Tam and those who agree with him, and so wrote many Aḥaronim, among them: Ben Ish Ḥai, Da'at Kedoshim. And although one could be lenient, since this is the opinion of most poskim and ḥalla in our times is rabbinic, le-khatḥila the custom is to be stringent, since there is no effort or loss in this.<br>When the flour or the dough was scalded in boiling water, such as cream puffs and large pretzels, as long as the boiling water did not prepare them for eating, but rather the baking prepared them for eating, they are considered a baked item and are obligated in ḥalla with a blessing (SA 329:8; and see Mishpetei Eretz 1:9; Divrei David 4, YD 49). On the other hand, if the cooking made the dough fit for eating, according to most poskim the baking afterward will not obligate it in ḥalla. Therefore, kugel is exempt from ḥalla, since already at the stage of cooking the noodles they are ready for eating, even though afterward their taste will be improved by baking them in a pot. Consequently, even if one establishes a meal over kugel, one does not recite \"Ha-motzi\" and Birkat Ha-mazon (based on the Ramban and Rabbi David, Pesaḥim 37a; Minḥat Shlomo vol. 3, 158:16; and unlike Rav Yosef Shalom Elyashiv, Kovetz Teshuvot 3:30; and see Mishpetei Eretz 1, note 27).<br>Kubbeh dough is exempt from ḥalla, because in addition to the fact that it is prepared by cooking, bulgur is mixed into it and this is not ordinary kneading (Binyan Av 4:60). If sufganiyot were prepared in a baking oven and not by deep-frying in oil, then of course according to all opinions one must separate ḥalla from the dough with a blessing."},{"n":13,"en":"When one intends to bake part, it is possible that in the end they will want to bake all of it, and therefore the Sages instituted that whenever one intends to bake part, the entire dough becomes obligated in ḥalla with a blessing (Taz 329:5; Shakh 6). And so the Maharam of Rothenburg instituted in order to resolve the doubt.<br>Some hold that one should separate ḥalla with a blessing already from the dough, since he intends to bake part of it (Baḥ). And some say only after its baking (Beit Yosef). Because of the doubt, one should separate after the baking.<br>If one intended to bake the thick dough, and afterward changed one's mind to cook it or deep-fry it in oil, according to all opinions one must separate ḥalla from it with a blessing, since at the time of its kneading one intended to bake it. All the more so in the reverse situation, when one kneaded the dough in order to cook it and changed one's mind and baked it, one must separate ḥalla from it with a blessing, since it is bread (SA 329:3)."}]},{"slug":"17-11-12","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/17-11-12/","title_he":"יב – עיסה נוזלית – בלינצ'ס ולחוח","title_en":"Liquid Batter – Blintzes and Laḥoḥ","body_en":"<p>As a rule, dough intended for baking is a thick dough, and by contrast, when one prepares a liquid batter for cooking, such as porridge and soups containing a lot of flour, they are exempt from ḥalla, and even if one eats from them the measure of a full meal, one recites \"Mezonot\" and \"Al Ha-miḥya.\"</p><p>However, sometimes the liquid batter is poured into a pot, a baking pan or a frying pan, and there it gradually becomes a baked item, such as ḥavitiyot (small pancakes). And since they have the form of bread, that is, the form of a baked item – one must separate ḥalla from them, and if one establishes a meal over them, one recites over them \"Ha-motzi\" and Birkat Ha-mazon. However, the obligation to separate ḥalla takes effect upon them only at the completion of their baking, for while they are batter they are not considered bread dough (Ha-teruma; Torat Ha-aretz vol. 1, 4:92-93). And one should note that even after baking they become obligated in ḥalla only if their flour contains the measure of obligation; and since usually a single baked item does not contain the measure obligating ḥalla, only if several baked items are combined together (as explained above in halakha 7) must one separate ḥalla from them.</p><p>The same is the law of pancakes, laḥoḥ, blintzes, Belgian waffles and the like: since they have the form of bread, that is, the form of a baked item – meaning that they have a thickness created by the rising of baking – they are obligated in ḥalla, and if one eats from them the measure of a full meal, one recites \"Ha-motzi\" and Birkat Ha-mazon. But if they were made especially thin, they do not have the form of a baked item and are exempt from ḥalla, and even if one eats from them the measure of a full meal, one recites \"Mezonot\" and \"Al Ha-miḥya.\" When there is a doubt whether they are regular or especially thin, one separates ḥalla without a blessing.<sup>14</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":14,"en":"Berakhot 37b: \"Trukanin are obligated in ḥalla … triyta is exempt from ḥalla.\" In both cases the subject is a liquid dough, but with trukanin a thick baked item is formed, and with triyta a thin baked item, and the Rosh explained that since it does not have the form of bread, it is not obligated in ḥalla. And so it is explained in SA 329:5 and in SA OḤ 168:15. The poskim grappled with the definition of the form of bread, and consequently with the law of pancakes, crêpes, laḥoḥ, blintzes and Belgian waffles, and many wrote that if they are thick they are obligated in ḥalla and if thin they are exempt. And one sought to establish that if their thickness is less than 3 millimeters they are exempt, and more than 7 they are obligated (Shulḥan Arukh Ha-mekutzar YD 170:19). And some say that if the liquid batter is poured into the pan and immediately becomes ready to eat, it is not in the form of bread and is exempt; and if it is thick and its baking continues for several minutes, it has the form of bread and is obligated in ḥalla (Tevu'at Ha-sadeh 5:14). In my humble opinion it seems that these are external signs on which halakha cannot be ruled, for one cannot propose that people measure the thickness of a pancake, and likewise the length of preparation also depends on the heat of the pan. Therefore one must say that as a rule all these baked goods are obligated in ḥalla, and only in exceptional cases, where they were made so thin that the form of bread, that is, the form of a baked item, is not visible in them because of their great thinness – are they exempt. And so we find in the poskim that a wafer is the common example of the triyta that is exempt, and the intention is a single very thin layer of the common wafer, which is crisp because of its great thinness and dryness, and not a Belgian waffle, which is thick."}]},{"slug":"17-11-13","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/17-11-13/","title_he":"יג – דינים שונים","title_en":"Various Laws","body_en":"<p><b>Ḥalla that became mixed into the dough</b>: If ḥalla was separated and then became mixed back into the dough, if the dough was a hundred times its amount, the ḥalla is nullified. If the dough was not a hundred times its amount, the dough is forbidden. And if they have not yet begun eating from the baked item, the one who separated the ḥalla can go to a Sage, who sits together with two other people as a beit din, and they annul for him the separation of the ḥalla retroactively. For the separation of ḥalla is like a vow, and just as one can annul a vow, so one can annul the separation of ḥalla (Rema 323:1). The one requesting annulment should say before them that he regrets having separated ḥalla, and had he known that it would become mixed into the dough he would not have separated it. And they say to him three times: \"It is permitted to you\" or \"The ḥalla is annulled,\" and thereby the dough or baked item becomes permitted, and he must separate ḥalla from it again without a blessing (Halakhot Ketanot 1:48; Birkei Yosef 323:2; Yabi'a Omer YD 1:21; and according to the Ḥatam Sofer YD 320, one should recite the blessing. The same law applies to terumot and ma'asrot, above 9:4, note 7).</p><p><b>Ḥalla outside the Land</b>: As we have learned (in halakhot 3-4), the Sages instituted separating ḥalla outside the Land as well, and even though at a time when the majority of Israel is not in the Land, the mitzva of ḥalla is rabbinic in the Land too, the mitzva of separation outside the Land is more lenient, since it is fundamentally rabbinic. The central expression of this is that in the Land of Israel one must separate ḥalla from the mukaf, that is, if one wishes to separate from one dough on behalf of another dough, when each of them contains the measure of ḥalla, both must be in the same place (as explained in halakha 7); but outside the Land one may separate even on behalf of dough that is not in the same place. Not only that, but it is permitted to bake the dough and begin eating from it, and only toward the end of eating it to separate ḥalla from it on behalf of what has already been eaten (SA 323:1).</p><p><b>Shabbat</b>: Just as it is forbidden to separate terumot and ma'asrot on Shabbat, so it is forbidden to separate ḥalla on Shabbat (SA OḤ 339:4; MB 26; above 10:4). One who is concerned that he will not manage to separate ḥalla before Shabbat begins can make a stipulation and say: \"What I will separate on Shabbat as ḥalla, behold it is ḥalla,\" and on Shabbat he recites the blessing and separates ḥalla, as explained above (10:4, note 1). Outside the Land, as we have learned, it is permitted to eat from the baked item and leave a little for after Shabbat, and separate ḥalla from it (Rema OḤ 506:3; MB 21).</p><p><b>Ḥalla of a gentile</b>: The dough of a gentile is exempt from the mitzva of ḥalla, and even if a Jew kneads the dough for him, the dough is exempt. Therefore, a bakery owned by gentiles, even if all the workers are Jews, is exempt from separating ḥalla. And unlike terumot, where if the gentile wishes to separate terumah his terumah becomes sanctified, with ḥalla, even if he separates, his ḥalla does not become sanctified (SA and Rema 330:1; 331:44; above 8:11). And one tells this to the gentile, so that he will know that there is no need to be particular that specifically kohanim eat the ḥalla he separated (Shakh 330:3). Dough in which a Jew and a gentile are partners – if the Jew's portion contains flour in the measure obligating ḥalla, the Jew's portion is obligated in ḥalla (SA 330:3).</p>","notes_en":[]}]});
